WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.465
Alright, let's get into it.

2
00:00:01.595 --> 00:00:02.705
We've all been there, right?

3
00:00:02.805 --> 00:00:05.105
Either you're the one sending a frantic message

4
00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:07.305
for tech help, or you are the one receiving it.

5
00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:10.145
And for translators, when a piece of software breaks,

6
00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:12.345
it's not just some small annoyance,

7
00:00:12.615 --> 00:00:14.265
it's a direct threat to a deadline.

8
00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:17.385
So imagine this message lands in your inbox.

9
00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:20.305
You can literally feel the panic coming through the screen.

10
00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:21.785
There's a project on the line,

11
00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:23.505
the clock is absolutely ticking,

12
00:00:23.765 --> 00:00:26.265
and this person is looking to you as their last hope.

13
00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.295
So what do you do? What's your gut reaction?

14
00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:31.095
Do you start firing back a bunch

15
00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:33.535
of possible solutions right away, or be honest?

16
00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:36.295
Do you feel a little bit of that panic yourself worrying

17
00:00:36.295 --> 00:00:37.295
that you won't know the answer?

18
00:00:37.885 --> 00:00:40.535
That first instinct is exactly what we need to talk about,

19
00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:43.255
and that is the whole secret right there.

20
00:00:43.705 --> 00:00:46.135
Being great at tech support isn't about being some kind

21
00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:49.615
of genius who has every single answer memorized, not at all.

22
00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:52.495
It's about having a calm, methodical system

23
00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:54.095
that lets you walk into that chaos

24
00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:55.815
and bring some order to it.

25
00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:58.415
First things first. Let's just acknowledge

26
00:00:58.415 --> 00:00:59.655
what these messages really are.

27
00:00:59.925 --> 00:01:01.895
They aren't just technical tickets in a queue.

28
00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:05.455
No, they're high stakes, often emotional situations

29
00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:08.335
and handling them well takes a pretty special mix of skills.

30
00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:12.575
You see, your role is way more than just the tech person.

31
00:01:12.915 --> 00:01:15.775
You're basically part detective, part therapist,

32
00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:18.135
and part guide, all rolled into one.

33
00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:19.575
You've gotta have the technical

34
00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:20.615
skills to figure out the problem.

35
00:01:20.725 --> 00:01:22.575
Sure, but you also need to understand

36
00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:24.015
how translators actually work,

37
00:01:24.435 --> 00:01:25.575
and you need the people skills

38
00:01:25.595 --> 00:01:28.135
to keep someone who's super stressed out, calm and focused.

39
00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:30.815
So how in the world do you juggle all of that?

40
00:01:31.285 --> 00:01:32.655
Well, you use a framework,

41
00:01:33.195 --> 00:01:35.055
and this isn't just some boring checklist.

42
00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:37.215
Think of it as your secret weapon.

43
00:01:37.675 --> 00:01:39.455
The tool that brings structure to the mess

44
00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:42.415
and gets you to a solution without wasting a bunch of time.

45
00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:46.545
And here it is. This is your roadmap.

46
00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:49.985
It's a really clear five step process that you can apply

47
00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:51.945
to just about any problem you come across.

48
00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:56.225
When you follow these steps in this order, you stop just

49
00:01:56.785 --> 00:01:59.185
reacting to the panic and you start strategically

50
00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.585
solving the actual problem.

51
00:02:01.275 --> 00:02:04.865
Let's break down how it works. Everything starts right here.

52
00:02:05.135 --> 00:02:07.625
That initial panicked message, it's broken.

53
00:02:08.085 --> 00:02:09.465
That's just the signal flare.

54
00:02:09.735 --> 00:02:11.105
Your number one job

55
00:02:11.285 --> 00:02:14.305
before you do anything else is to get past the emotion

56
00:02:14.605 --> 00:02:16.665
and find out what is really going on.

57
00:02:17.755 --> 00:02:19.525
This slide is the perfect example

58
00:02:19.625 --> 00:02:22.565
of why asking good questions is so powerful.

59
00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:25.565
A user tells you, send slash receive is broken.

60
00:02:26.035 --> 00:02:28.125
Okay, what does broken actually mean?

61
00:02:28.545 --> 00:02:30.165
It could mean the button is grayed out,

62
00:02:30.465 --> 00:02:32.605
or maybe it just spins and never finishes.

63
00:02:32.945 --> 00:02:35.005
Or maybe a specific error message pops up.

64
00:02:35.545 --> 00:02:38.005
See each of those specific issues points

65
00:02:38.025 --> 00:02:39.485
to a completely different cause

66
00:02:39.585 --> 00:02:40.965
and a totally different solution.

67
00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:43.285
Your questions are the tools that turn

68
00:02:43.285 --> 00:02:45.525
that vague complaint into something you can actually fix.

69
00:02:46.315 --> 00:02:48.845
Okay? So once you've got a clear picture of the symptom,

70
00:02:49.465 --> 00:02:50.885
you can start to isolate the cause.

71
00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:52.565
Is this something the user did

72
00:02:52.565 --> 00:02:54.245
or is it a real bug in the software?

73
00:02:54.665 --> 00:02:56.285
Is it only happening on their computer

74
00:02:56.345 --> 00:02:58.125
or is everyone on the shared project seeing it?

75
00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.045
Is the problem problem with the software itself

76
00:03:00.045 --> 00:03:01.805
or is it an issue with their specific data

77
00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:03.165
like a corrupted project file?

78
00:03:03.465 --> 00:03:05.685
Or hey, could it just be a spotty internet connection?

79
00:03:06.065 --> 00:03:08.245
Asking these questions helps you shrink the world

80
00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:10.045
of possibilities down to something manageable.

81
00:03:10.835 --> 00:03:13.295
Now, having a slick technical framework is awesome,

82
00:03:13.395 --> 00:03:15.015
but it's really only half the battle.

83
00:03:15.635 --> 00:03:17.135
How you communicate while you're going

84
00:03:17.135 --> 00:03:19.175
through these steps can make all the difference in the

85
00:03:19.175 --> 00:03:21.055
world, especially when someone's deadline

86
00:03:21.055 --> 00:03:22.095
is hanging in the balance.

87
00:03:23.145 --> 00:03:24.555
Just think about the impact

88
00:03:24.615 --> 00:03:26.955
of a sentence like this right away.

89
00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:28.275
You've shown them, you get it,

90
00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:30.395
you've acknowledged their stress, you validated

91
00:03:30.395 --> 00:03:32.795
that it's urgent, and you framed it as a partnership.

92
00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:34.475
Let's figure this out together.

93
00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:37.035
You're not just some tech support person anymore.

94
00:03:37.055 --> 00:03:39.955
You're an ally. That one little shift can bring the tension

95
00:03:39.965 --> 00:03:41.595
level way, way down.

96
00:03:42.645 --> 00:03:44.545
You know what builds a ton of trust?

97
00:03:45.385 --> 00:03:48.385
Honesty, pretending you know everything is a surefire way

98
00:03:48.385 --> 00:03:49.385
to get yourself into trouble.

99
00:03:49.715 --> 00:03:52.105
Being upfront and saying, you know, this is a new one

100
00:03:52.105 --> 00:03:54.345
for me, but I have a process to figure it out, is

101
00:03:54.405 --> 00:03:56.425
so much more reassuring than just faking it.

102
00:03:57.195 --> 00:03:58.895
And this is how you keep that trust going.

103
00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:01.095
You have to keep the user in the loop.

104
00:04:01.325 --> 00:04:02.375
Tell them what you're doing

105
00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:03.575
and when you'll get back to them.

106
00:04:03.725 --> 00:04:06.815
Something as simple as, okay, I need to investigate this.

107
00:04:06.925 --> 00:04:09.055
Give me about an hour and I'll update you, is

108
00:04:09.055 --> 00:04:10.575
so much better than just going silent.

109
00:04:10.885 --> 00:04:13.855
Silence is where a user's anxiety really starts to spiral.

110
00:04:14.675 --> 00:04:17.175
So you're following the steps, you're communicating well,

111
00:04:17.755 --> 00:04:18.815
but what if you get stuck?

112
00:04:19.245 --> 00:04:21.455
This brings us to a really important part of the process

113
00:04:21.605 --> 00:04:23.095
that people sometimes get wrong.

114
00:04:23.445 --> 00:04:25.015
Knowing when to escalate.

115
00:04:25.625 --> 00:04:28.855
These are The red flags. If you've tried all the usual

116
00:04:28.905 --> 00:04:30.975
Fixes and you're getting nowhere, it's time.

117
00:04:31.595 --> 00:04:32.735
If you even suspect

118
00:04:32.735 --> 00:04:34.015
Something like data corruption,

119
00:04:34.165 --> 00:04:35.165
It's time. If

120
00:04:35.165 --> 00:04:37.615
the solution requires admin rights, you don't have,

121
00:04:37.915 --> 00:04:39.935
or if it's just plain beyond what you know,

122
00:04:40.285 --> 00:04:41.455
it's time to escalate.

123
00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.215
And honestly, if That deadline is breathing down their

124
00:04:44.215 --> 00:04:45.655
neck and you're still just investigating,

125
00:04:45.955 --> 00:04:47.895
that's also a good time to bring in reinforcements

126
00:04:48.445 --> 00:04:51.565
That please let this one really sink in.

127
00:04:51.975 --> 00:04:54.205
Escalating is not a sign that you failed.

128
00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:57.805
It is a strategic professional decision you make

129
00:04:58.105 --> 00:05:00.045
to protect the user and their work.

130
00:05:00.585 --> 00:05:01.965
It shows that you have good judgment

131
00:05:02.305 --> 00:05:04.605
and in the long run, it protects everyone involved.

132
00:05:05.435 --> 00:05:07.255
So let's tie all of this together.

133
00:05:07.725 --> 00:05:10.695
When you combine that systematic framework with clear,

134
00:05:11.175 --> 00:05:14.415
empathetic communication, you can step into pretty much any

135
00:05:14.415 --> 00:05:17.735
technical crisis and confidently move the situation from

136
00:05:17.735 --> 00:05:18.895
chaos to control.

137
00:05:19.755 --> 00:05:21.545
Think of this as your new muscle memory.

138
00:05:21.965 --> 00:05:23.225
The panic message comes in.

139
00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:25.385
Your old instinct might be to jump in with answers.

140
00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:28.985
Your new instinct, you pause, you ask questions

141
00:05:29.125 --> 00:05:30.225
before you diagnose.

142
00:05:30.565 --> 00:05:33.345
You test one single thing at a time so you actually know

143
00:05:33.345 --> 00:05:36.105
what worked and all along the way you communicate.

144
00:05:36.455 --> 00:05:37.465
This simple loop is

145
00:05:37.465 --> 00:05:38.865
what truly separates the good

146
00:05:38.865 --> 00:05:40.465
troubleshooters from the great ones.

147
00:05:41.315 --> 00:05:44.215
So the next time one of those messages lands in your inbox,

148
00:05:44.635 --> 00:05:46.815
you won't feel that same wave of panic

149
00:05:47.085 --> 00:05:50.415
because you'll have a system, you'll have a framework.

150
00:05:50.875 --> 00:05:54.095
The only question left is what's the first clarifying

151
00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:55.215
question you're gonna ask.
